{
  "dom_id": "step-q343191",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14211452/assets/69b4b3c2488cf394df687d7446ab5a49bab037de936402f02d43196f71dddf09.png)\n\nTwo glass plates are held a constant distance apart, forming a growing sheet of laminated glass resin. As rollers pull the plates apart, the length is increasing by $2\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min},$ while the $1\\,\\text{cm}$ thickness does not change. If resin is injected so that the volume of the sheet is increasing at a rate of $32\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/min},$  how fast is its width changing when its dimensions are $10\\,\\text{cm}$ in length and $4\\,\\text{cm}$ in width?\n\nThe width is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{cm}/\\text{min}.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =10\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ y & =4\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ z & =1\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =32\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=10\\,\\text{cm},$ $y=4\\,\\text{cm}$ and $z=1\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nSince $z$ is not changing and $z=1,$ we get\n\n$$\nV=xy.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(xy) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =x(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+y(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}32 & =10(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+4(2) \\\\ 32 & =10(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+8 \\\\ 24 & =10(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2.4\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the width of the solid is increasing at a rate of $2.4\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}$ at the moment when $x=10\\,\\text{cm},$ $y=4\\,\\text{cm}$ and $z=1\\,\\text{cm}.$",
